WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.470
In 1982, Ridley Scott's tech-noir classic
Blade Runner was met with skeptical audiences

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:10.080
and empty theaters, thanks in part to a series
of slapdash changes made after low test screening

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.700
scores panicked the studio.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.730
But now alternative versions of the movie
— more in line with the director's original

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.270
vision — are widely available to curious
viewers.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:21.850
As a result, the film's popularity has risen
steadily over the years, along with a lot

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:22.850
of burning questions.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.630
"Are you for real?"

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.360
No, seriously: is he?

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.000
Blade Runner depicts a near future in which
androids — known as replicants — are so

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.989
advanced that they're virtually indistinguishable
from the real thing.

00:00:32.989 --> 00:00:36.800
And most of the debate surrounding the film
centers on whether its hero Rick Deckard,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.510
played by Harrison Ford, is a human or a replicant.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.790
Let's try to make some sense of it all.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.100
The Origami Unicorn

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.089
So, is replicant-hunter Rick Deckard actually
an android?

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:49.930
This scene suggests the answer is a definite
yes.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:54.429
After dreaming of a unicorn earlier in the
film, Deckard finds and fondles this origami

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:59.280
version — which must have been made by Eduardo
Gaff, the veteran LAPD blade runner and origami

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.100
maker who orders Deckard around.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.810
But Deckard isn't exactly the type to open
up to his coworkers about his nighttime fantasies...

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:09.471
which means the only way Gaff could possibly
know Deckard dreams about unicorns is that

00:01:09.471 --> 00:01:11.720
he's somehow read Deckard's memory file.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.409
And if there's one thing we learn throughout
this movie, it's that...

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:16.990
Gaff is creepy

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.729
Who delivers Deckard's LAPD summons at a dirty
noodle bar?

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:21.729
Gaff.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:25.190
Who creeps up behind Deckard as soon as he
retires the first of his assigned targets?

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.190
Gaff.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:30.670
Who shows up immediately after Nexus 6 replicant
Roy Batty empties the contents of his soul

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:31.700
in the pouring rain?

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:32.700
Gaff!

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.479
And who knows all about Deckard's secret unicorn
dreams?

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:37.310
C'mon, don't make us say it again.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.710
It's pretty clear that LAPD veteran Gaff is
monitoring Deckard.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.149
But why would this so-called "one-man slaughterhouse"
need a leash?

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:48.710
Maybe because he's a replicant...only like
the film's femme fatale, Rachael, he doesn't

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.729
know it.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:50.869
Who's the man?

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:55.330
Eduardo Gaff also drops one major hint that
the our blade runner hero might not be human

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:59.600
after all, after he's completed his mission
in a surprising, climactic moment.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.950
"You've done a man's job, sir."

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.399
This is one of the most dissected, debated
lines in the film's history, and for good

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:10.289
reason: if you want to praise a guy for a
job well done, it's kind of a weird choice

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:11.370
of words, right?

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.760
That is, unless the man in question isn't
a man at all, but a robot, in which case it's

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.570
a heck of a compliment… and a good way to
tip off your audience as to Deckard's true

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:22.209
nature, in case they hadn't figured it out
yet.

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:23.790
The director's take

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.349
At this point, the evidence seems to be piling
up that Deckard is, indeed, a replicant — and

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:31.430
we haven't even mentioned how he survives
through multiple battles that would probably

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.659
leave an ordinary person in several pieces.

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:37.260
Or the split-second where his eyes seemed
to flash with the otherworldly glow that's

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.290
unique to the film's replicant characters.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.690
But since nobody knows the truth better than
the man who made the movie, it probably makes

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.400
the most sense to just ask him.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.739
Ridley Scott has already said that when it
comes to the Deckard question, "If you don't

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:49.210
get it, you're a moron."

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:53.420
But in an interview with Wired, he went into
detail for all us morons, saying:

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.049
"That's the whole point of Gaff, the guy who
makes origami and leaves little matchstick

00:02:57.049 --> 00:02:58.049
figures around.

00:02:58.049 --> 00:03:00.239
He doesn't like Deckard, and we don't really
know why.

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:04.310
If you take for granted for a moment that,
let's say, Deckard is a Nexus 7, he probably

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.549
has an unknown life span and therefore is
starting to get awfully human."

00:03:08.549 --> 00:03:09.640
But wait, there's more

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:14.879
So, Rick Deckard is a replicant, has always
been a replicant, and will always be a replicant,

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:15.879
right?

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:16.879
Well... not so fast.

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:20.940
The dissenting opinion on that front is none
other than Harrison Ford, who says he and

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.019
Ridley Scott were always at odds over Deckard's
true nature.

00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:28.480
"So I resisted the idea of being a replicant."

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.900
Ford felt that Blade Runner's audience should
have a human character to connect with — and

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.209
in the original theatrical cut of the film,
which was missing some of the key scenes that

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:39.090
brought Deckard's humanity into question,
Ford's interpretation makes more sense.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:43.160
But when the director says Deckard is a replicant,
and the guy who plays Deckard says he's not,

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.650
it's hard to know who to believe… until
you remember that Deckard is ostensibly a

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.709
robot who only thinks he's a person, which
means that Ford's insistence otherwise could

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:52.860
just be a genius bit of method acting.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.329
The more he shares his character's delusions,
the realer the film.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:57.329
Right?

00:03:57.329 --> 00:03:58.329
Right?!

00:03:58.329 --> 00:03:59.769
The question is the answer

00:03:59.769 --> 00:04:03.830
Pretty much everyone involved in Blade Runner
has an opinion on the is-he-or-isn't-he debate

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:07.459
about Rick Deckard — but author Philip K.
Dick, who wrote the book the movie was based

00:04:07.459 --> 00:04:09.110
on, should get the last word.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:13.750
And according to Dick, the ambiguity surrounding
Deckard's humanity — and more broadly, surrounding

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.239
whether there's any real difference between
humans and replicants — is kind of the whole

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:18.239
point.

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:21.650
It's a replicant who saves Deckard's life
and delivers the film's most poignant lines

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.650
before dying with grace and dignity.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:27.120
When the replicants show more empathy than
the men who created them, which one of them

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.120
is more human?

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.260
What do the insights of a robot tell us about
the meaning of existence?

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.979
"They're just questions, Leon."

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:37.840
In other words, Blade Runner is designed to
make you think bigger, about the nature of

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.040
life itself — whether you're human, or something
more.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.040
Thanks for watching!

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.210
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.560
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

